{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_jdzxzkspnmjgftrxy55onsukhtsoojflcnomne3us6suqgzovvoq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Real divergence found — exactly the padding-propagation issue. C's struct assignment copies all 16 bytes (two 8-byte `mov`s), but Rust's `*b = *a` leaves the padding word untouched, so the destination keeps stale padding from the earlier `memcpy`:\n\n```\ninput a[0].pad = 0x80000001   a[1].pad = 0x81010101\nC    a[0].pad = 0x81010101  ← padding travelled with the element\nRust a[0].pad = 0x80000001  ← padding stayed behind\n```\n\nLet me fix the Rust to copy the full struct footprint like the C does."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1400,"cache_read_input_tokens":56360,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1400,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1fde8561-ba9e-456f-aab5-e839e9993055","uuid":"8bc3e115-e5d9-41ac-9616-62ed3cec4318","timestamp":"2026-08-26T23:57:51.610Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_jdzxzkspnmjgftrxy55onsukhtsoojflcnomne3us6suqgzovvoq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01DyKtCjTWqnQcYfJpGnyksJ","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-5x6dJI/translation/src/lib.rs","old_string":"/// ```c\n/// static void spritebatch_internal_merge_sort_iteration(\n///     spritebatch_sprite_t *a, int lo, int split, int hi, spritebatch_sprite_t *b);\n/// ```\nunsafe fn spritebatch_internal_merge_sort_iteration(","new_string":"/// Byte-exact equivalent of the C struct assignment `*dst = *src`.\n///\n/// `spritebatch_sprite_t` has 4 bytes of trailing padding (offsets 12..16). A\n/// plain Rust `*dst = *src` is NOT equivalent to the C assignment: rustc/LLVM\n/// treat padding as `undef` and only copy the two real fields, so the\n/// destination keeps whatever padding it already had. GCC, by contrast, compiles\n/// `b[k] = a[i]` into two 8-byte `mov`s that span all 16 bytes, so the padding\n/// travels with the element and is observable in the output buffers.\n///\n/// `copy` (memmove semantics) rather than `copy_nonoverlapping` is used because\n/// the caller may alias `a` and `b` (`merge_sort(p, p, n)`); GCC's sequence\n/// loads both halves into registers before storing either, which is exactly\n/// memmove behaviour for a 16-byte overlap.\n#[inline]\nunsafe fn sprite_assign(dst: *mut spritebatch_sprite_t, src: *const spritebatch_sprite_t) {\n    core::ptr::copy(\n        src as *const u8,\n        dst as *mut u8,\n        core::mem::size_of::<spritebatch_sprite_t>(),\n    );\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// static void spritebatch_internal_merge_sort_iteration(\n///     spritebatch_sprite_t *a, int lo, int split, int hi, spritebatch_sprite_t *b);\n/// ```\nunsafe fn spritebatch_internal_merge_sort_iteration("}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1400,"cache_read_input_tokens":56360,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1400,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1fde8561-ba9e-456f-aab5-e839e9993055","uuid":"cdc38746-5595-415f-a382-8cf11e607739","timestamp":"2026-08-26T23:57:58.875Z"}
