{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_cgvgerkrgzz75bfeuvodtagz3or22dqu3zzol7xpwjkzrl2pkilq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete — **all checks pass**. Summary:\n\n## What the library is\n`c_src` is one translation unit exporting a single symbol, `merge_sort` (a ping-pong merge sort over `spritebatch_sprite_t`), plus three `static` helpers.\n\n## Two real bugs found and fixed in the Rust\n\n**1. Struct padding wasn't propagated** (all profiles). `spritebatch_sprite_t` is 16 bytes with 4 bytes of trailing padding. GCC compiles `b[k] = a[i]` into two 8-byte `mov`s spanning all 16 bytes, so padding travels with the element. Rust's `*b = *a` treats padding as `undef` and copied only the two real fields, leaving stale padding behind:\n\n```\ninput   a[0].pad=0x80000001  a[1].pad=0x81010101\nC    →  a[0].pad=0x81010101   ← padding moved with the element\nRust →  a[0].pad=0x80000001   ← stayed behind (WRONG)\n```\n\n**2. `copy_nonoverlapping` aborted where C succeeds** (debug-only, so release hid it). When a caller aliases the buffers (`merge_sort(p, p, n)`), the no-overlap precondition fires and the process aborts — the C returns normally. Fixed by binding libc `memcpy` via `extern \"C\"` and calling it unconditionally, exactly as the C does. Now verified at the instruction level: C `cltq; shl $0x4; call memcpy` vs Rust `movslq; shl $0x4; call memcpy`.\n\nI found #2 only because I ran the suite against **both** the release and debug `.so` — worth noting since profile is not a cargo feature and is easy to skip.\n\n## Deliverables\n- `SYMBOLS.md` / `ERRORS.md` (20 rows) / `CONFIGS.md` (30 rows), all derived by grepping the C\n- 49 tests across `phase_b_configs.rs` (30), `phase_c_errors.rs` (16), `phase_d_symbols.rs` (3) — both `.so`s loaded via `libloading`, never calling Rust directly\n- `verify.sh` — symbol diff × 3 feature combos × 2 profiles = 6 configs, all green\n- `mutation_check.sh` — 11 mutants × 2 profiles, **22/22 caught**\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n- **`ERRORS.md` #12: the C's `texture_id` tiebreak is dead code.** `if (a->sort_bits <= b->sort_bits) return 1;` already covers `==`, so line 9 is unreachable and `texture_id` never affects ordering. I replicated this rather than fixing it, and `err12_13` pins it as a property (inputs differing only in `texture_id` must permute identically) so nobody \"fixes\" it later.\n- **2 of 20 error rows are documented rather than tested**, because they're structurally unreachable: #17 needs a caller lying about `size`, and #19 needs ≥2^30 elements (17 GB). Every other row has a passing test.\n\nOne process note: during mutation testing my source backup was wiped because `$TMPDIR` is not stable between tool calls here, which briefly left `src/lib.rs` mutated. I restored it and confirmed via SHA-256 (`1b2671e6…`) plus a line-by-line diff against the C; `mutation_check.sh` now keeps its backup inside the crate and re-verifies the checksum before exit. `c_src/` was never modified (mtimes still predate the session)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1620,"cache_read_input_tokens":157105,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1620,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1fde8561-ba9e-456f-aab5-e839e9993055","uuid":"43e029a0-7128-4296-bad6-ead98546bcde","timestamp":"2026-08-27T00:17:03.483Z"}
